/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  acetonePlant  --  ACETONE FROM 2-PROPANOL, THE CLOSED FLOWSHEET
  ============================================================================

  W. L. Luyben, "Design and Control of the Acetone Process via Dehydrogenation
  of 2-Propanol", Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 50 (2011) 1206-1218, DOI
  10.1021/ie901923a -- his Figure 1 (the Turton base case), end to end:

      fresh feed + IPA recycle -> vaporiser -> reactor -> separator
                                            -> absorber (offgas washed)
                                            -> C1 -> acetone product
                                                  -> C2 -> water product
                                                        -> IPA RECYCLE (tear)

  This is the case the six preceding ones were built to make possible.  Each of
  them isolates one unit on Luyben's OWN published inlet, so that whatever it
  gets wrong is that unit and its thermodynamics.  Here nothing is published
  except the fresh feed: every internal stream is the plant's own answer, and
  the errors compose.

  WHAT THIS CASE CLAIMS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.  It claims to be Luyben's
  TOPOLOGY and Luyben's EQUIPMENT SPECIFICATIONS solved on the thermodynamics
  Choupo actually has.  It does NOT claim to reproduce his stream table -- the
  siblings measured, in advance, exactly why it cannot, and the gap between
  his table and this one is the deliverable rather than a defect to be closed:

    * acetone01: UNIFAC puts the IPA/water azeotrope at x 0.590 against his
      0.673, and the lake-estimated isopropanol boils 4.5 K high.
    * acetone04: UNIFAC INVENTS an acetone/water azeotrope at x 0.978 where
      his UNIQUAC has none -- 0.044 K deep, and decisive.
    * acetone06: so C1 caps at 97.3 mol % acetone against his 99.9 % spec.
    * acetone07: so C2's recycle caps at x_IPA 0.580 against his 0.650, and
      11.6 % of the isopropanol entering it leaves in the water product.

  Both column ceilings were WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE the columns existed and are
  pinned by check_azeotrope_ceiling.  This case is where they stop being two
  separate findings and become one plant.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DEPARTURES FROM HIS FIGURE 1, each argued rather than absorbed
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

  (a) NO HYDROGEN IN THE COLUMNS.  His C1 has a partial condenser and a vent
      (0.0465 kmol/h, carrying 0.0130 kmol/h H2 and 0.0335 kmol/h acetone);
      Choupo's column has a total condenser and no vapour distillate.  A
      permanent gas fed to a total condenser is asked to leave as a liquid,
      and the engine rightly refuses (hydrogen has no liquid heat capacity at
      330 K).  So the separator's LIQUID goes to C1 and the hydrogen leaves
      with the offgas, i.e. the columns are modelled downstream of his vent.
      Cost, stated: the 0.0335 kmol/h of acetone his vent loses is not lost
      here, which flatters this plant's acetone recovery by 0.10 %.

  (b) THE SEPARATOR DOES THE COOLING.  An isothermal flash at 318 K computes
      the duty to bring its feed there, so this one unit stands for his HX1
      plus his separator and its Q is directly comparable to his 0.8993 MW.
      A separate `heater` was tried in acetone03 and refused, correctly:
      Choupo's heater takes a DUTY and reports the temperature, because a
      heater with a specified outlet temperature is a design specification.

  (c) THE SEPARATOR PRESSURE IS ARGUED, NOT FITTED.  Luyben states 1.5 atm for
      the absorber gas, which is downstream; there is no compressor between
      reactor and separator and pressure only falls, so the separator sits at
      the reactor's 2.6 atm.  acetone03 records the sweep that would have
      tempted a fitted value and why it was refused.

  (d) THE ABSORBER'S SECOND OUTLET REJOINS C1's FEED, as in his Figure 1
      (his F1 of 72.85 kmol/h is the flash liquid AFTER the absorber bottoms
      of 20.05 kmol/h rejoin it).

  (e) NO VENT/PURGE ON THE RECYCLE LOOP.  Luyben has none either -- the loop
      closes through C2's overhead and nothing else -- so this is his
      structure, not a simplification.  It does mean any component that both
      enters and cannot leave would accumulate without bound; here water
      leaves in three places and isopropanol in two, so the loop is closed
      and bounded.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        //  THE RECYCLE MIXER.  `ipaRecycle` is BOTH an input here and C2's
        //  output far below -- the flat plant-level tear pattern, declared in
        //  system/solverDict.  The sequential-plan contract refuses an
        //  undeclared backward edge by name, which is what makes this
        //  readable rather than lucky.
        //
        //  Its T is DECLARED, not solved.  A genuine cold-liquid mix whose
        //  species carry a large formation+latent gap on the gas basis makes
        //  the adiabatic single-phase T-solve ill-posed (the same call hda
        //  makes and for the same reason).  It only labels the stream: the
        //  vaporiser below sets the real reactor-feed state, and its duty is
        //  measured from THERE.
        name        M1;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( freshFeed  ipaRecycle );
        outputs     ( mixedFeed );
        operation   { T 320 K; }
    }

    {
        //  Luyben's VAPORISER: he quotes 0.7577 MW of LP steam to bring the
        //  reactor feed to 389 K at 2.6 atm.  Here the outlet temperature is
        //  the SPECIFICATION and the duty is the RESULT, so Q_kW is directly
        //  comparable to his figure and is one of the few numbers in this
        //  plant that can be.
        name        vaporiser;
        type        phaseChanger;
        in          mixedFeed;
        outputs     ( Rin );
        operation   { outletT 389 K;  P 2.6 atm; }
    }

    {
        //  His reactor: 90 % per-pass conversion of isopropanol at 623 K.
        //  A conversionReactor and not a pfr, deliberately -- his kinetics are
        //  transcribed in the design record and deliberately unused, because
        //  the paper gives no tube dimensions and a pfr cannot be dimensioned
        //  from what is in hand.  acetone02 records that decision.
        name        reactor;
        type        conversionReactor;
        in          Rin;
        outputs     ( Rout );
        operation   { conversion 0.90;  T 623 K; }
        reaction    dehydrogenation;
    }

    {
        name        separator;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        in          Rout;
        outputs     ( flashLiquid  flashGas );
        operation   { T 318 K;  P 2.6 atm; }
    }

    {
        //  9 stages, ID 0.217 m, 1.5 atm (his spec).  Washes the acetone out
        //  of the hydrogen-rich offgas with 20 kmol/h of water.  acetone05
        //  measured this unit in isolation and found it recovers a SIXTH of
        //  what the paper does -- that finding rides into this plant as a
        //  larger acetone loss in the offgas, and is not re-argued here.
        name        absorber;
        type        absorber;
        inputs      ( flashGas  washWater );
        outputs     ( offgas  absBottoms );
        operation   { stages 9; }

        //  ---------------------------------------------------------------
        //  THE MODEL BOUNDARY, and it is not decoration -- the plant does
        //  not run without it.
        //
        //  Handed the plant's gammaPhi/UNIFAC world, this unit REFUSES by
        //  name: "this package names no SOLVENT, so the unit cannot tell
        //  which component washes and which is washed out".  It is right.
        //  `absorber` solves Kremser, Kremser reads HENRY'S LAW, and an
        //  activity model carries no answer it can use.  Before 2026-08-12
        //  it did not refuse -- it silently deleted the wash-water stream
        //  and exited 0.
        //
        //  So the absorber gets its OWN world: water the solvent on the
        //  Raoult rung, acetone the solute on the Henry rung, hydrogen and
        //  isopropanol inert.  This is the settled heterogeneous-thermo
        //  architecture doing the job it was built for, on a published
        //  flowsheet rather than on a demonstration: components stay global,
        //  models are replaced for this unit only.
        //
        //  WHAT IT COSTS IS VISIBLE, NOT HIDDEN.  The rule is `H` conserved,
        //  `T` a model-dependent readout: crossing a model boundary holds
        //  (T, P, z) and lets the enthalpy JUMP, and the jump is printed.
        //  The model-boundary ledger prices that step independently -- it
        //  assembles both worlds itself and credits the step only if its own
        //  arithmetic reproduces the imbalance -- so the first-law report on
        //  this unit separates the RAW residual, the DECLARED step, and what
        //  REMAINS.  A student can read exactly what changing thermodynamics
        //  mid-flowsheet cost, in kilowatts.
        //
        //  AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT INFORMED THIS UNIT DOES NOT REACH IT.
        //  acetone04 measured UNIFAC's acetone/water surface in order to
        //  inform the absorber, and the absorber consumes none of it.  That
        //  correction is acetone05's, recorded there and not re-argued: the
        //  measurement stands, because it is the right surface for the two
        //  COLUMNS, which do take their K from the activity model.
        thermo
        {
            equilibrium
            {
                formulation diluteSolution;

                liquid
                {
                    solvent
                    {
                        component     water;
                        standardState pureLiquid;
                    }
                    solutes
                    {
                        components    ( acetone );
                        standardState infiniteDilution;
                        solutionModel henryDilute;
                        binaryParameters
                        {
                            acetone-water { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/acetone-water.dat"; }
                        }
                    }
                }

                vapour { fugacityModel idealGas; }
            }
        }
    }

    {
        name        M2;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( flashLiquid  absBottoms );
        outputs     ( F1 );
        operation   { T 325 K; }
    }

    {
        //  ---------------------------------------------------------------
        //  THE VENT, and it is here because the plant refused to run without
        //  it -- a structural departure paid for by a measurement, not a
        //  preference.
        //
        //  Luyben's C1 has a PARTIAL condenser and vents 0.0465 kmol/h of
        //  non-condensables off the top.  Choupo's column has a total
        //  condenser and no vapour distillate, so the hydrogen dissolved in
        //  the separator's liquid has nowhere to go -- and it is not
        //  harmless.  Measured, on this plant's own F1 (probe, 2026-08-13):
        //
        //     with H2 at 6.8e-4     rigorous MESH BLOWS UP: |F| 6.2e+12
        //                           after 3 iterations, x_D(IPA) NEGATIVE
        //     the same feed, no H2  converges, 8 iterations, |F| 2.2e-14
        //
        //  at every distillate rate tried (24, 26, 27, 32.25 kmol/h).  A
        //  supercritical permanent gas at K ~ 1e3-1e4 wrecks the Jacobian.
        //
        //  AND THE HYDROGEN THAT REACHES HERE IS ITSELF AN ARTEFACT OF A
        //  DECLARED APPROXIMATION.  H2 runs at gamma = 1 outside the activity
        //  model (constant/thermoPhysPropDict authorises it by name, because
        //  no UNIFAC group for hydrogen exists and inventing one would be
        //  fabrication).  acetone03 already recorded that this is "right for
        //  the wrong reason": the separator sends H2 almost entirely to the
        //  vapour, which is correct, but via a vapour pressure extrapolated
        //  far above hydrogen's 33.18 K critical temperature.  The residue it
        //  leaves in the liquid -- 6.8e-4 here against Luyben's 2e-4 -- is
        //  that approximation's error, and it is large enough to break the
        //  unit three steps downstream.  **That is the clearest thing this
        //  plant has to teach**: a declared approximation with a stated,
        //  small local effect was not locally contained.
        //
        //  So the vent is an explicit unit rather than a deletion.  It is a
        //  flash at the column's own pressure: V/F = 0.16 %, and what leaves
        //  with the hydrogen is COMPUTED, not assumed, and is directly
        //  comparable to his 0.0465 kmol/h vent and its 0.0335 kmol/h of
        //  acetone.  Placed on the FEED rather than on the condenser, which
        //  is where this engine can express it -- said plainly, because it is
        //  a different place from his.
        name        vent;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        in          F1;
        outputs     ( columnFeed  ventGas );
        operation   { T 325 K;  P 1 atm; }
    }

    {
        //  C1: 66 stages, feed 54, RR 2.78 -- all his.  acetone06 predicted
        //  and then measured this column's 97.3 % acetone ceiling on Luyben's
        //  own feed; here it gets the plant's, which is poorer.
        //
        //  THE DISTILLATE RATE IS NOT HIS, AND THE RULE IS STATED RATHER THAN
        //  THE NUMBER FITTED.  His 32.25 kmol/h is the acetone in HIS feed:
        //  his C1 sends essentially all of it overhead at 99.9 %.  This
        //  plant's C1 receives only ~26.6 kmol/h of acetone, because the
        //  separator sends 2.9x too much of it to the vapour (acetone03) and
        //  the absorber recovers a sixth of what the paper does (acetone05).
        //  Asked for 32.25 it would have to make up the balance with water.
        //
        //  So the SPEC is carried over rather than the number: send overhead
        //  the acetone that arrives, at the azeotropic purity acetone04
        //  measured and acetone06 confirmed --
        //
        //      D = (acetone reaching C1) / x_D,ceiling ~ 26.6 / 0.973 = 27.3
        //
        //  and it is rounded DOWN to 27.0, because at 27.45 (all the acetone,
        //  no water) the MESH pinches and does not converge in 80 iterations.
        //  That last step is a design iteration performed by the author and
        //  shown here rather than hidden: the number was not tuned to improve
        //  agreement with the paper -- it cannot, it is 16 % below his rate,
        //  which is the finding.
        name        C1;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       simultaneous;
        in          columnFeed;
        outputs     ( acetoneProduct  B1 );
        operation
        {
            nStages 66;  feedStage 54;  refluxRatio 2.78;
            distillateRecovery { component acetone;  fraction 0.97; }
            feedQuality 1.0;  P 1 atm;
        }
    }

    {
        //  C2: 19 stages, feed 16, RR 0.849, 5.88 kmol/h overhead -- and that
        //  overhead is the tear.  acetone07 predicted and then measured its
        //  x_IPA 0.580 ceiling against his 0.650.
        name        C2;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       simultaneous;
        in          B1;
        outputs     ( ipaRecycle  waterProduct );
        operation
        {
            nStages 19;  feedStage 16;  refluxRatio 0.849;
            distillateRate 5.88 kmol/h;  feedQuality 1.0;  P 1 atm;
        }
    }
);

boundary
{
    inlets   ( freshFeed  washWater );
    outlets  ( acetoneProduct  waterProduct  offgas  ventGas );
}

//  stream state lives on disk in 0/ (one file per stream); this dict is
//  TOPOLOGY only.
